From 8ac40e32d45b2de32e11f2fb51f7f24fbacceb33 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LuneOS <luneos@webos-ports.org>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 17:30:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] notification_manager: post to com.webos.notification

1.6.0 added notification forwarding to org.freedesktop.Notifications on the
session bus. LuneOS does not provide that name, so the service skipped itself
on every session start and Android notifications went nowhere. Notifications
on webOS belong to com.webos.notification, over luna-service2, and it offers
several kinds rather than one - so the kind is chosen from what Android said
about the notification instead of flattening everything into a toast:

  transient        -> createToast, a banner that passes and leaves nothing
                      behind, which is what transient means
  has actions      -> createAlert, the only kind with buttons, one per action
  everything else  -> createNotification, which persists in the list and
                      carries the sound and urgency flags

Each kind is closed differently - closeToast, closeAlert, removeNotification
- so what was created is remembered alongside its id, and replacing a
notification closes whatever it replaces first.

Attribution matters as much as delivery: sourceId is the webOS application id
generated for that Android app, waydroid.<package>, so a notification carries
the app's own name and icon rather than arriving as an anonymous system
message, and tapping it raises that app.

Two details that only showed up against a live notificationmgr. It rejects an
empty message outright, with CA_MSG_EMPTY, and some notifications carry
neither a body nor a summary - so the app's name is used rather than posting
something that will be thrown away. And it silently substitutes its own
generic alert icon for an iconUrl it cannot read, so the icon is only offered
when Waydroid has actually extracted one for that package; notifications from
the system come from packages that have none.

Android identifies a notification by an integer and webOS by a string, so the
two are mapped here. image_data is raw pixels while webOS wants a URI, so the
extracted icon stands in for it.

A button's onclick is a luna-service2 URI that notificationmgr calls
directly, and it cannot re-enter this process - so a button raises the app
rather than invoking that particular Android action, and no listener is
called back. Routing a real action back into Android needs a service on this
side to receive it, which is worth doing separately.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [LuneOS specific]
